program            = 002_mixed_int_double_promotions
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Default argument promotions across the variadic boundary: float to double and narrow integers to int, folded and volatile-runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Header exception: this program includes <stdarg.h>. The corpus sanctions two header
exception classes and only two, and this is the first of them: the programs in area 07 may
include <stdarg.h> and nothing else. The second class is the dedicated bundled-header probe
12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c, which may include the nine required bundled
freestanding headers; it does not apply here, and no program may take both. The exception is
needed because variadic functions cannot be written at all without
va_list, va_start, va_arg and va_end. This program uses exactly those four; va_copy sits
on the same documented row but is not needed here, because no list is copied and none is
traversed twice. stdarg.h is in bcc's own bundled
freestanding set (docs/technical-specifications.md line 208) and is also a freestanding
header supplied by the reference compiler, so it compiles identically under both oracles
and the program remains a single-file reproducer. No other header is included. stdio.h
is not included because bcc ships none, and stdbool.h is not included because _Bool is a
C11 keyword written directly. printf is hand-declared.
Promoted retrieval types, the central correctness rule of this program: an argument of
type float undergoes the default argument promotions when passed through an ellipsis and
arrives as double, so it is retrieved with va_arg(ap, double) and never with
va_arg(ap, float). Arguments of type signed char, unsigned char, short, unsigned short
and _Bool are promoted to int, so they are retrieved with va_arg(ap, int) and never with
their unpromoted types. Retrieving a variadic argument with a type other than its
promoted type is undefined behaviour and would invalidate both differential oracles,
because a divergence between two compilers on an undefined program proves nothing about
either. Every narrow-integer value is produced by an explicit cast, and the cast sits in a
different place in each variant: at the call site in the folded variant, and in the
initializer of the volatile source object in the runtime variant, from which the value
reaches the call through a same-type assignment. No implicit narrowing occurs anywhere.
Variadic discipline: va_start names count in sum_doubles and sum_promoted_ints, and
names n_flt in mixed_args, in each case the last named parameter. Each helper retrieves
exactly as many arguments as its call site supplies, so no va_list is read past its
arguments. Each va_start has exactly one matching va_end in the same function. No
va_copy and no va_list forwarding appear here.
Arithmetic: no signed overflow is possible. The narrow-integer totals are 57196 for the
folded variant and 32895 for the runtime one, both far inside the 32-bit int range. The
folded variant uses interior values, while the runtime operands are deliberately the exact
minimum and maximum values of their narrow types - -128, 255, -32768 and 65535 - which is
where a sign- or width-extension defect in the promotion path would show, and every one of
them is representable in int. Floating addition is on
dyadic rationals only. There is no shift, no division, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, and no
dependence on padding or on the addresses of unrelated objects.
Evaluation order: main stores each returning helper's result in a named temporary before
printing it, so no call has more than one side-effecting argument. In the runtime
variants each volatile object is read by a single simple assignment into a plain local of
the same type before the call, so the arguments in the call expression are pure reads of
plain locals and the unspecified argument-evaluation order is unobservable. Keeping the
volatile object and the plain local at the same type means the assignment performs no
conversion, and the promotion happens at the call, which is exactly what is under test.
Warning gate: no deviation is claimed and no ub_audit_flags key is present, which is the
way this format spells "the full default gate applies". That is not a convenience: the
program was put through the whole gate, -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion
-Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror, at every optimization level of its sweep and with the
native driver and all three cross drivers, and it produced no diagnostic at all. The
sanitizer gate, -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all, was also built and
run, and it terminated cleanly with an empty diagnostic stream and the stdout recorded
below. Since a deviation is expressed only as a removal from that fixed gate, and this
program needs no removal, stating the gate here would assert a narrowing that was never
made.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Why the unsigned narrow types promote to int rather than unsigned int: int is 32 bits on
all four targets. That width is measured with each target's own reference driver rather
than assumed, because the repository's per-target table records pointer, long, ELF class
and endianness but not the width of int. With a 32-bit int the whole value range of
unsigned char (0 to 255) and of unsigned short (0 to 65535) is representable in int, so
the integer promotions convert both to int and va_arg(ap, int) is the correct retrieval
type on every target. Using va_arg(ap, unsigned int) would be wrong on all four.
Width normalization: the types long and unsigned long are never used, because sizeof(long)
and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64 - the widths the
per-target table in docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457 to 462 records, confirmed
here by measurement. Only int, signed char, unsigned char, short, unsigned short, _Bool,
float and double appear, and the printf conversions are limited to %s, %d and %.6f. No
address, no pointer value and no sizeof result is printed.
Plain char signedness: plain char is signed on x86-64 and i686 and unsigned on AArch64
and RISC-V 64. This program therefore never uses bare char as a value type. It uses
explicit signed char and unsigned char, so the measured difference cannot affect the
output. Character pointers appear only as const char * tag strings.
Floating determinism: every floating literal in the program is a dyadic rational exactly
representable in IEEE binary32 and in binary64 - the argument values 1.5, 2.25, 4.125,
0.25, 8.5, 3.0625, 1.25, 2.5 and 0.75, together with the accumulator seed 0.0 - so every
sum is computed exactly and no rounding difference can arise between backends. All
floating output is printed at fixed precision with %.6f, so the rendered text is
byte-identical on all four backends.
Cross-backend comparability: integer and double variadic argument passing was measured to
produce byte-identical results on all four targets at all three optimization levels, so
no target is restricted and all three oracles are enabled. This record therefore narrows
nothing, and these notes are recorded as the reasoning behind that, not as the reason for
an exclusion.
END
